WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.769
Pings work across the cloud, but big file transfers hang. The cause is a 50 byte VXLAN header and an MTU mismatch.

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:11.898
Pause and think.

00:00:12.298 --> 00:00:15.317
How many bytes does VXLAN over IPv4 add?

00:00:15.717 --> 00:00:23.031
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:30.760
This video explains VXLAN, the overlay that lets cloud virtual networks span many physical hosts.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:44.627
New word: V X L A N. Virtual Extensible LAN: wrapping network packets inside UDP to build virtual networks across data centres. Picture it like this: Putting a letter inside a bigger envelope to cross a border.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.041
We cover the packet wrapping, the M T U math, and how the forwarding table learns.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:01:00.887
New word: M T U. Maximum Transmission Unit: the largest packet size a network link carries. Picture it like this: The height limit of a tunnel.

00:01:03.287 --> 00:01:05.446
Think of mailing a letter through a courier.

00:01:05.846 --> 00:01:09.364
Your letter is the original Ethernet frame from a virtual machine.

00:01:09.764 --> 00:01:17.624
The courier puts it inside a larger envelope with a label saying which customer network it belongs to. That label is the V N I.

00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:27.505
New word: V N I. VXLAN Network Identifier: the 24-bit ID of a virtual network. Picture it like this: The colour of the envelope.

00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:34.602
The courier's truck only takes packages up to a fixed size. That size is the M T U.

00:01:35.002 --> 00:01:39.483
Because the envelope adds size, your letter must be smaller to still fit.

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:41.566
Four words.

00:01:41.966 --> 00:01:45.542
The V N I is a twenty four bit number naming the tenant network.

00:01:45.942 --> 00:01:51.422
A V TEP is the endpoint that wraps and unwraps frames, a host or a smart NIC.

00:01:51.587 --> 00:02:02.048
New word: N I C. Network Interface Card: the hardware that connects a computer to a network. Picture it like this: The mailbox and postman's slot of a house.

00:02:03.648 --> 00:02:08.199
The M T U is the biggest packet a link carries, often fifteen hundred bytes.

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:12.964
The F D B maps a MAC address to the remote V TEP that owns it.

00:02:13.048 --> 00:02:25.680
New word: M A C. Media Access Control address: a network card's hardware address; also Message Authentication Code. Picture it like this: The serial number stamped on the mailbox.

00:02:28.048 --> 00:02:29.453
Let's add up the cost.

00:02:29.853 --> 00:02:32.047
Fourteen bytes of outer Ethernet header.

00:02:32.447 --> 00:02:34.351
Twenty bytes of outer IPv4.

00:02:34.751 --> 00:02:37.886
Eight bytes of UDP, on port forty seven eighty nine.

00:02:37.948 --> 00:02:49.941
New word: U D P. User Datagram Protocol: fast, connectionless packets with no delivery guarantee. Picture it like this: Throwing postcards: fast, but some may get lost.

00:02:51.541 --> 00:03:10.546
Eight bytes of VXLAN header with the V N I. The outer Ethernet header doesn't count against the underlay's IP M T U, so the inner Ethernet header is charged instead, and the total is still fifty bytes: on a fifteen hundred byte underlay the inner IP M T U is fourteen fifty. Get this wrong and large packets silently vanish.

00:03:10.741 --> 00:03:24.185
New word: I P. Internet Protocol address (a network address); also Intellectual Property — a licensed chip design block. Picture it like this: A street address for a computer (or a licensed blueprint for a chip part).

00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:27.064
Recap.

00:03:27.464 --> 00:03:30.889
VXLAN carries an Ethernet frame inside UDP.

00:03:31.289 --> 00:03:33.251
The V N I keeps tenants apart.

00:03:33.651 --> 00:03:37.819
Lower the inner M T U by fifty, or use jumbo frames underneath.

00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:43.304
The F D B learns which V TEP owns each MAC and forgets stale entries.

00:03:43.704 --> 00:03:46.595
Lesson thirty eight point two builds this in C.

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:49.073
Back to the question.

00:03:49.473 --> 00:03:52.491
How many bytes does VXLAN over IPv4 add?

00:03:52.891 --> 00:03:54.180
About 50 bytes.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:56.251
Quick recap.

00:03:56.651 --> 00:03:59.704
VXLAN wraps Ethernet inside UDP.

00:04:00.104 --> 00:04:02.066
Leave MTU room for the header.

00:04:02.466 --> 00:04:05.392
The forwarding table learns where MACs live.
